<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="freelancing-business"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>The best business ideas for students in India sell a skill you already have to someone who already pays for it: video editing, design, social media help for local shops, tutoring and campus services.</strong>
    Start with a phone or laptop, not a loan, and check your age and the platform rules before you take a rupee. A student business that builds real proof of work becomes part of a high-value skill portfolio, which supports better income options and earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Twelve ideas below, each with a label: Scalable, Scalable with a business layer, or Not so scalable. The first four sell a skill you can package and later share with helpers.</li>
      <li>Under 18, a parent or guardian should be the one who signs with customers. Minors' agreements are treated as void, and bank, tax, GST, food and platform rules each have age limits.</li>
      <li><strong>Your next step: pick one idea, name one customer you can message today, and run a small paid test before you spend any study money.</strong></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Most lists give you twelve ideas and stop. They skip the part that decides whether you can actually do it at 16, 17 or 19: who signs, which platforms accept your age, what the tax rule is, and which "earn from home" offers take your money.
  </p>
  <p>
