    <p>
      <strong>Verdict:</strong> for most students the best first business is a skill-led service for local customers, not a product with stock. It costs the least, teaches the most about sales and gives you proof you can use in a job or internship search later.
    </p>
    <p>
      Competition is real. Many students offer the same thing, so a generic "I do designing" will lose to someone who says "I make menus for cafes in this area". Aim narrow. Resale and low-skill gig work are crowded, with thin margins and nothing owned at the end, so keep a second layer growing beside them.
