  <h2 id="study-balance">Fitting a business around studies</h2>

  <p>
    Study comes first because the exam is the gate to your next stage. Treat the money set aside for your education as off limits. A college seat should take only a small share of that budget (we use about 10 percent as a starting guide), and the rest has to stay free for living, tools and learning. So never fund a business test from fees, hostel money or a loan.
  </p>

  <div class="reality-grid">
    <div class="reality-card"><div class="reality-card__title">Class 10 or 11</div><p>Treat it as a practice project. Choose the idea that needs the least equipment and the least travel, and let a parent see every message with a customer.</p></div>
    <div class="reality-card"><div class="reality-card__title">Class 12 and entrance season</div><p>Pause new customers. Keep one repeat client if it costs little time. A skill built now still counts later, but a rank cannot be redone.</p></div>
    <div class="reality-card"><div class="reality-card__title">College years 1 and 2</div><p>This is the best time to test. Marks pressure is lower, you have campus customers and time to fail cheaply.</p></div>
    <div class="reality-card"><div class="reality-card__title">Final year</div><p>Use the business as proof for job applications: three finished jobs and a customer line beat another certificate. Keep placement dates in your calendar.</p></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Set fixed slots in the week and drop them before exams. A small number of steady hours beats a burst that collapses in a month. Some students find a rhythm in a few weeks, others need a couple of months, and both are normal. Check your college's rules on attendance and outside work, since they differ. A business does not replace a degree where one is legally needed, such as medicine, law or teaching posts. For most other careers, skills come first and the degree second, and a small business is a cheap way to build the skills.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sales-basics">Sales basics every student can learn</h2>

  <p>
    A business is mostly selling. You do not need a sales course. You need these basics, which carry into any job or career later.
  </p>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    <li><strong>Positioning.</strong> Say who you help and with what, in one sentence. "I make menus for cafes in this area" beats "I design."</li>
    <li><strong>A simple offer.</strong> One result, one price, one delivery date, in three lines.</li>
    <li><strong>Proof.</strong> Three finished examples and one line from a real customer.</li>
    <li><strong>Follow-up.</strong> Most students send one message and quit. Send a polite second message with something new, such as a new sample.</li>
    <li><strong>Referrals.</strong> Ask right after a good result: "Who else do you know who needs this?"</li>
    <li><strong>The price talk.</strong> When someone says "let me think", ask what is unclear. Do not cut the price first. Explain what the problem costs them now.</li>
    <li><strong>One number to watch.</strong> Count messages sent, replies and paid yeses. If replies are low, fix the message or the offer. Also compare what it costs you to win a customer, in time and money, with what that customer pays over repeat jobs.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Put scope, price and delivery date in writing before you start, ask for a part payment, and price any extra request as a new job. Write down how you do each job as a short checklist while you do it. That checklist is what lets a friend or an AI tool help you later, and it is what turns a gig into a business. Keep money in two places: one account for personal use, one for the business, and pay yourself a fixed amount. Do not let one app, one marketplace or one customer be your only source of work. Keep your own list of customers' contact details, with their permission, so an account ban or an algorithm change cannot erase your income.
  </p>

