      <li>Does the offer need me to pay before I earn? If yes, stop.</li>
      <li>Is the money mainly made by bringing in more people? If yes, stop.</li>
      <li>Can I check the seller's claims with someone who has no reason to flatter them? If no, wait.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ai">Using AI without faking your work</h2>

  <p>
    AI helps students deliver faster: captions, first designs, practice questions, listings and plain explanations. Because everyone can do the same, speed alone no longer earns trust. What a customer pays for is that you checked the result, knew the local context and replied fast.
  </p>

  <div class="stage-list">
    <div class="stage"><div class="stage__num">1</div><div><div class="stage__title">Now</div><p>Use AI for first drafts. Check every fact, name, price and number yourself. Keep a note of what it got wrong.</p></div></div>
    <div class="stage"><div class="stage__num">2</div><div><div class="stage__title">Next</div><p>Build one repeatable workflow for your service, such as a caption plan or a menu layout, and note the time it saves.</p></div></div>
    <div class="stage"><div class="stage__num">3</div><div><div class="stage__title">Later</div><p>As tools improve, the students who can direct them, verify output and explain the result will be worth more. No multiplier is guaranteed.</p></div></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Using AI is not a proof asset by itself. "I used AI to make a menu, then checked it with the owner and fixed three errors" is.
  </p>

  <h2 id="owner-mindset">The owner's mindset, and why it matters even if you take a job</h2>

  <div class="reality-grid">
    <div class="reality-card"><div class="reality-card__title">Ownership over salary</div><p>A salary is a tool for saving and learning. Wealth comes from owning part of something: a business, a product or an asset. A student business is the cheapest place to practise ownership.</p></div>
    <div class="reality-card"><div class="reality-card__title">Show your work early</div><p>Put your work on one platform from the start: Instagram, LinkedIn, a simple page. Customers, internships and mentors find people who show proof.</p></div>
    <div class="reality-card"><div class="reality-card__title">Think big, start small</div><p>Think about the studio or brand you want in ten years. Then do the next small job well. Big goals keep your small choices honest.</p></div>
    <div class="reality-card"><div class="reality-card__title">Skills over certificates</div><p>A pile of certificates is not a skill portfolio. One core skill, one multiplier skill (such as English, sales or data) and visible proof make the high-income skill portfolio that opens better options. Clear English, a few good contacts and basic AI and tech comfort help in every idea here.</p></div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Pay for learning only when it adds feedback, practice or access that free material cannot. Information is free on YouTube, official help pages and reputable low-cost courses. Judge the instructor, last update, exercises and refund terms before paying, and never let course fees eat the money you need to live and learn.
  </p>

